TestNG - Test New Generation
java based unit testing tool

selenium only web automation interacting with elements adding verification is not enough
need to have test cases and validate and execute together

adv :
        1. test cases and test suites
        2. grouping of test cases(divide in to multiple grps)
        3. prioritize
        4. parameterize
        5. parallel testing
        6. report

    adding testNG library from maven repository link : https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/org.testng/testng
    copy the dependency add to pom.xml

    in testNG there is no main method
    it has @Test annotation (kind of replacement to main method) test method in the class

    to open testNG reports (test-output files/folders) - > run -> edit configurations
    On the left side, look for Templates (or default configurations) and select TestNG.
    Open the Listeners tab.
    Check the box that says "Use default reporters". -> Click Apply.

    creating testNg.xml file
    1. suite file
    2. inside test file/files
    3. inside classes inside classes class file/files name

    for ref:
    <suite name="DefaultSuite" verbose="2">
        <test name="LoginTest">
            <classes>
                <class name="basic.basicLoginImpl"/>
            </classes>
        </test>
    </suite>

    annotations:
    @Test

    @BeforeMethod -> runs only when before execution of any test method in the class
    @AfterMethod  -> runs only when after execution of any test method in the class
--------------------------------------------------------------------

    @BeforeClass  -> executes only before starting of the class (inside class there are methods - test methods)
    @AfterClass   -> executes only after ending of the class (inside class there are methods - test methods)

--------------------------------------------------------------------

    // according to testNG.xml -> it's the collection of classes is called one test
    @BeforeTest
    @AfterTest

    work at test entry level
    <suite name="DefaultSuite" verbose="2">   -------> before suite
        <test name="LoginTest">     ------> before test
            <classes>               ------> before class
                <class name="basic.basicLoginImpl"/>  -----> before method |  test | after method
            </classes>             -------> after class
        </test>                    -------> after test
    </suite>                       -------> after suite

--------------------------------------------------------------------

    // imp for @BeforeTest and @AfterTest

    1. if there is only one class then before test and after test will execute only once not everytime
    example 1: here we have only one class in both test so before test and after test will work only once.

    <suite name="DefaultSuite" verbose="2">
        <test name="forTest1">
            <classes>
                <class name="testHandling.test1"/>
            </classes>
        </test>

        <test name="forTest2">
            <classes>
                <class name="testHandling.test2"/>
            </classes>
        </test>

    </suite>
--------------------------------------------------------------------
    2.It will only executes before test and after test when there are more than one class in the test
    example 2: here we have more than one class in both tests so before test and after tests have worked for all the tests. (here 2 tests so 2 times).

    <suite name="DefaultSuite" verbose="2">
        <test name="forTest1">
            <classes>
                <class name="testHandling.test1"/>
                <class name="testHandling.test2"/>
            </classes>
        </test>

        <test name="forTest2">
            <classes>
                <class name="testHandling.test2"/>
                <class name="testHandling.test1"/>
            </classes>
        </test>

    </suite>
--------------------------------------------------------------------
    @BeforeSuite
    @AfterSuite

    suite will be from top to till bottom
    <suite name="DefaultSuite" verbose="2">   -----------> BeforeSuite
        <test name="forSuite1">
            <classes>
                <class name="suiteHandling.suite1"/>
                <class name="suiteHandling.suite2"/>
            </classes>
        </test>
    </suite>                                  ------------> AfterSuite

